Date: Fri, 29 Apr 2022 15:39:35 +0000
Subject: Can left-truncation distances vary by object and observer?
Message-ID: <AbxhgsNBlOs.4dLAloANAgAJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/distance-sampling/c/AbxhgsNBlOs/m/4dLAloANAgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Good day list,

We conducted a helicopter-based double-observer (independent observers) survey of polar bears where detection on the line was not certain from either platform (front vs rear seats in the helicopter). Front bubble windows allowed front observers to see animals on the line (if not obscured by e.g., vegetation or glare). Detection by rear observers was hindered by a blind spot - rear observers could not see out the front of the helicopter or directly beneath it. Varying crosswinds required the pilot to rotate the helicopter varying angles into the wind in order to hold a straight flight line - this caused the location of the blind spot to shift to the left and right, such that rear observers on one side could sometimes detect animals very close to the line, but on the other side of the transect, animals quite far from the line would be in the blind spot. Below, the dashed line is both the transect and the direction of travel and the grey triangle represents the blind spot. During the survey, front observers noted when they detected animals that were likely not available for detection by rear observers based on a combination of distance, side, and angle of the helicopter relative to the transect (true for 14 of 127  detected objects). Below, only the bear on the left would have been marked as not available to rear observers.

I tried providing a matrix with columns "left" and "width" and one row for each object x observer combination as the "int.range" argument of the ddf() function to specify different left-truncation distances for different objects and observers. Below "flat.io <http://flat.io>" is the data file with two rows for each detected object (one row per observer). "left" is always 0 for the front observer, but varies for the rear observer (set to > the observed distance to objects that fell in the blind spot).

> trunc = cbind(flat.io <http://flat.io>$left, flat.io <http://flat.io>$width)

> test.io.pi = ddf(    dsmodel = ~mcds(formula = ~1, key = "hn"),
            mrmodel = ~glm(~distance), data = flat.io <http://flat.io>, method = "io",
            meta.data = list(int.range = trunc))

Warning in process.data(data, meta.data) :
  no truncation distance specified; using largest observed distance
Error in integratepdf(ddfobj, select = !x$binned, width = right, int.range = intrange[!x$binned,  :

 Number of integration ranges (int.range) does not match number of observations

Error in if (control$debug) { : argument is of length zero

These warnings seem to suggest that (1) distances must be binned into intervals for this approach to be used, and (2) the lengths of the columns in int.range should match the number of objects, not the number of objects x observers, i.e., truncation distances can vary among objects but not between observers of an object. This also seems consistent with the documentation for the ddf function.

Is it possible/appropriate to specify different left-truncation distances for different observers (as well as different objects) in an MRDS model?

If not, any suggestions on how to cope with this issue would be appreciated.

We will evaluate covariates of detection (platform, side, vegetation height and density, visibility, etc) in both the MR and DS models. Modelling the effect of platform might be sufficient here (?), but it seems preferable to also explicitly account for limited availability for detection by the rear observers if possible.

Thanks in advance, and sorry for the length of this post,

Eric

