References: <AbxhgsNBlOs.4dLAloANAgAJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/distance-sampling/c/AbxhgsNBlOs/m/y8R2o0gPAgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

This quotation from Laake et al. (2008) Coping with variation in aerial survey protocol for line-transect sampling seems relevant here.

"It is important to remember that the analysis methods we presented all hinge on the assumption that g(x0) = 1 for all of the conditions encountered. ... g(x0) ... can be considerably less than unity for [some surveys]. However, the ideas and methods we have presented here can be easily extended for double-observer aerial line transects to enable estimation of detection probability on the line and how it might vary with changes in altitude, speed, observer and a host of other factors that can vary during the course of a survey. The added advantage is that the type of left truncation for the blind strip is no longer important because detection probability at the left edge is measured directly with double-observer methods."

This makes me think that including platform (front vs rear), distance, and the interaction between platform and distance in the MR model could be effective here.

Eric

